For the 9-box analysis, we look at dealer performance across three different vintage windows:
Last 12 months: the latest 12 fully matured vintages.
Last 3 months: the latest 3 fully matured vintages.
Forward-looking window: open vintages that may still develop early losses.
For example, if we run the analysis in October, the latest fully matured vintage is 7 months behind, as we need a 7-month observation window to assess performance. Using the current example, the 12-month window would cover Mar-25 to Feb-26, while the latest 3-month window would cover Dec-25 to Feb-26. The forward-looking window would then cover Mar-26 to Jun-26, including one additional month to capture all contracts currently under observation.
For each window, we assess dealers using two indicators:
ECO Rate by Amount = ECO amount / total financed amount of the dealer.
This measures the level of early credit losses within the dealer’s own portfolio.
ECO Concentration = dealer’s ECO amount / total ECO amount in the country.
This measures how much each dealer contributes to the country’s overall ECO exposure. A large dealer, for example, may have a moderate ECO rate but still represent a significant share of total ECOs.
We then classify dealers into three bands for each indicator.
ECO Concentration ranking:
Chile: Top 10 / Rank 11–20 / Rank >20
Colombia: Top 5 / Rank 6–15 / Rank >15
ECO Rate by Amount:
<1.0%
1.0%–1.3% 
>1.3%

The 9-box grid is built separately for each vintage window, with:
Columns: ECO Rate by Amount
Rows: ECO Concentration ranking
This gives us three 9-box views: Last 12M, Last 3M, and the forward-looking/open-vintage window.
 
Finally, we compare the dealer’s position across the three views to prioritize follow-up actions. For example, if a dealer that consistently appears in the highest-risk area across all three views would indicate a more persistent risk pattern, while a dealer that moves into the highest-risk area only in the open-vintage view, particularly with a significant ECO amount, may represent an emerging risk that requires early attention.
